From: nxrs contributors
Subject: [PATCH RFC] Xtensa: align hardware-loop bodies after final layout

Draft proposal, not in the evaluation series and not activated in an SDK.
Branch relaxation can invalidate padding selected by hardware-loop fixup.
Keep structural loop repair early and add a final alignment-only pass.
Reserve a worst-case LOOP plus padding size for branch and literal ranges;
emit valid two/three-byte NOPs outside the repeated body. Align to a word
boundary so four-byte ESP32-S3 instructions are also conservatively covered.

Functions with inline assembly use the existing software-loop lowering;
assembly directives and labels do not have reliably estimated byte sizes.
Explicit MIR hardware loops combined with assembly are rejected, not guessed.
The final offset walk honors bounded block alignment and stronger alignments.
Native-object tests check alignment and independently decoded destinations.
This remains a proposal pending Rust-driver and firmware qualification.

